1
00:00:01,868 --> 00:00:03,244
It's funny, isn't it?

2
00:00:03,369 --> 00:00:05,622
Dead cars are quite beautiful
in their way

3
00:00:06,331 --> 00:00:08,666
whereas dead people...
not so much.

4
00:00:09,834 --> 00:00:12,670
{\an8}I suppose if we were cars this place
would be a horrific Charnel house.

5
00:00:13,254 --> 00:00:15,798
{\an8}- It's too early for philosophy.
- Never too early.

6
00:00:16,424 --> 00:00:17,800
- Hey, Cho!
- That's what I'm talking about...

7
00:00:17,926 --> 00:00:19,427
Hey boss. She's over this way.

8
00:00:28,686 --> 00:00:32,190
Hey, we've got an unidentified female
somewhere in her late teens, early 20s.

9
00:00:32,690 --> 00:00:34,776
Oh, wow. A little notice
would've been nice.

10
00:00:34,984 --> 00:00:36,986
Be grateful it's wintertime.
It could be a lot worse.

11
00:00:37,737 --> 00:00:40,156
Looks like she was bludgeoned multiple times
around her left temple.

12
00:00:40,281 --> 00:00:42,575
There's some serious blunt force trauma
to the back of her head as well.

13
00:00:42,700 --> 00:00:44,911
Somebody wanted her good and dead.
How long has she been down?

14
00:00:45,078 --> 00:00:46,454
From the decomposition of the body

15
00:00:46,579 --> 00:00:47,956
she's probably been out
here at least a week.

16
00:00:48,665 --> 00:00:50,792
- Is that the guy who found her?
- Yeah, a scavenger.

17
00:00:51,417 --> 00:00:53,002
Came across the victim
when he was combing through the van.

18
00:00:53,461 --> 00:00:56,464
It looks like somebody took something off
of her after she was already here.

19
00:00:56,714 --> 00:00:59,133
A watch or a bracelet, I'm guessing.
Left the earrings though.

20
00:00:59,759 --> 00:01:01,594
Found a set of tracks over there.
I think they're connected.

21
00:01:01,761 --> 00:01:03,596
It's a salvage area.
There'd have to be lots of tracks.

22
00:01:04,013 --> 00:01:05,390
Yeah, it's a long shot

23
00:01:05,515 --> 00:01:07,392
but there weren't many tracks in the area,
and these look pretty fresh.

24
00:01:12,939 --> 00:01:14,732
- Smells like transmission fluid.
- That's what I thought.

25
00:01:14,857 --> 00:01:16,234
I'll have forensics run some tests.

26
00:01:16,442 --> 00:01:18,569
Now if a vehicle with a leaking
transmission made the track

27
00:01:18,736 --> 00:01:20,697
then the driver couldn't have gone
far without service.

28
00:01:22,448 --> 00:01:24,867
- Hey, what's... what's his name?
- Franklin.

29
00:01:24,993 --> 00:01:26,369
- Cool, it boy.
- May I?

30
00:01:26,494 --> 00:01:27,870
I have a way with dogs.

31
00:01:28,246 --> 00:01:30,540
Hey, Franklin. How are you?

32
00:01:30,999 --> 00:01:32,375
Yeah, you're a good boy.

33
00:01:34,127 --> 00:01:35,586
I thought you said
you had a way with dogs.

34
00:01:35,712 --> 00:01:37,088
He wanted freedom.

35
00:01:37,755 --> 00:01:40,216
- Franklin! Heel!
- Keep up, Lisbon!

36
00:01:42,135 --> 00:01:43,511
Franklin!

37
00:01:45,388 --> 00:01:46,764
What's that?

38
00:01:46,889 --> 00:01:48,850
Franklin, come. Come on now.

39
00:01:49,767 --> 00:01:51,144
Get out of there, Franklin.

40
00:01:53,021 --> 00:01:54,397
It's a foot!

41
00:02:00,153 --> 00:02:01,988
Another body. Are you done?

42
00:02:02,613 --> 00:02:03,990
Hey, what do you say, Franklin?

43
00:02:04,115 --> 00:02:05,491
Are we done yet?
Huh, we done?

44
00:02:19,128 --> 00:02:21,213
The cadaver dogs didn't find
any more bodies, did they?

45
00:02:21,380 --> 00:02:22,757
Thankfully, no.

46
00:02:22,882 --> 00:02:24,258
The test confirmed there was a lot
of transmission fluid

47
00:02:24,383 --> 00:02:25,760
in that track at the scene.

48
00:02:26,177 --> 00:02:28,012
And the tire pattern only fits
a few specific van models.

49
00:02:28,346 --> 00:02:30,014
{\an8}I'm checking garages just to see
if any vehicles

50
00:02:30,139 --> 00:02:31,974
{\an8}fitting the description were turned
in with transmission problems

51
00:02:32,099 --> 00:02:33,476
{\an8}over the last week.

52
00:02:33,601 --> 00:02:35,061
{\an8}All right.
Cho, you mind helping him out?

53
00:02:38,272 --> 00:02:39,648
{\an8}- Is there a problem here?
- No.

54
00:02:39,774 --> 00:02:41,859
{\an8}- No, ma'am.
- Great. I see a name.

55
00:02:42,068 --> 00:02:43,444
{\an8}Did we I.D. the victims?

56
00:02:43,569 --> 00:02:45,696
{\an8}- Only on one.
- Got a hit on her prints.

57
00:02:45,821 --> 00:02:47,198
It's for the second victim you found.

58
00:02:47,615 --> 00:02:49,950
{\an8}Her name was Valerie Bestin.
No next of kin.

59
00:02:50,576 --> 00:02:52,912
{\an8}She was arrested four years ago
after hitting a homeless man

60
00:02:53,037 --> 00:02:54,413
{\an8}while driving drunk.

61
00:02:54,538 --> 00:02:55,915
{\an8}The M.E. puts her time of death
within the last 24 hours.

62
00:02:56,040 --> 00:02:58,209
{\an8}Bestin was bludgeoned in the same
manner as the first victim.

63
00:02:58,793 --> 00:03:00,169
{\an8}What about an I.D.
on the other victim?

64
00:03:00,503 --> 00:03:01,879
Her prints haven't come up yet.

65
00:03:02,296 --> 00:03:04,256
The M.E. pegs her as Latina, early 20s.

66
00:03:04,382 --> 00:03:06,967
{\an8}Do a preliminary search
of the missing persons database.

67
00:03:07,093 --> 00:03:08,677
{\an8}- Maybe we'll get lucky.
- Yes, ma'am.

68
00:03:08,886 --> 00:03:11,180
{\an8}All right, we've got two victims...
one Caucasian

69
00:03:11,305 --> 00:03:12,681
the other probably Latina.

70
00:03:12,807 --> 00:03:14,392
{\an8}At least a 10-year age
gap between them

71
00:03:14,517 --> 00:03:16,936
{\an8}and judging by their clothes,
Valerie had more money.

72
00:03:17,144 --> 00:03:19,688
{\an8}But both were bludgeoned
and dumped in the same place

73
00:03:19,814 --> 00:03:21,899
{\an8}one week after the other.
What's the connection?

74
00:03:22,108 --> 00:03:24,443
{\an8}Library, bowling alley, nail salon.
Could be anything.

75
00:03:24,860 --> 00:03:26,320
Well, let's focus on
what we do know.

76
00:03:26,445 --> 00:03:28,364
{\an8}What about the homeless guy
Bestin hit?

77
00:03:28,489 --> 00:03:30,783
{\an8}He's been in a secure mental facility
since last year.

78
00:03:31,033 --> 00:03:33,160
{\an8}Speaking of mental facilities,
have you seen Jane?

79
00:03:33,452 --> 00:03:34,829
{\an8}I need to talk to him.

80
00:03:38,416 --> 00:03:39,792
{\an8}There you are!

81
00:03:43,087 --> 00:03:44,463
{\an8}Where's my couch?

82
00:03:44,588 --> 00:03:47,508
{\an8}Oh. Yes.
I bought you a new one.

83
00:03:47,967 --> 00:03:49,510
- I like my old couch.
- No, you don't.

84
00:03:50,052 --> 00:03:51,429
{\an8}You've gotta try this one.

85
00:03:51,554 --> 00:03:53,055
{\an8}Let's discuss it later.
We have to talk.

86
00:03:53,180 --> 00:03:54,557
- Have you ever been into knitting?
- What?

87
00:03:54,723 --> 00:03:56,725
No, of course not.
You grew up surrounded by boys.

88
00:03:56,851 --> 00:03:58,227
What are you talking about?

89
00:03:58,352 --> 00:03:59,728
Hi there. You seem to have
very nimble fingers.

90
00:03:59,854 --> 00:04:01,230
Are you now or have you
ever been a knitter?

91
00:04:01,480 --> 00:04:02,982
No. Agent Lisbon.

92
00:04:03,107 --> 00:04:04,900
Yes, Dr. Montague.
It's a pleasure to meet you.

93
00:04:05,651 --> 00:04:07,820
{\an8}- And you must be Patrick Jane.
- I must be.

94
00:04:08,571 --> 00:04:09,947
{\an8}You were saying we needed to talk.

95
00:04:10,072 --> 00:04:12,324
Yes, Dr. Montague is consulting
on the case.

96
00:04:14,076 --> 00:04:16,078
Yes, she's a decision science consultant.

97
00:04:16,203 --> 00:04:17,997
She works with the FBI
and state agencies.

98
00:04:18,122 --> 00:04:20,249
Her work helped bring
in the quarry killer.

99
00:04:21,292 --> 00:04:23,377
I'm developing a predictive
computer model.

100
00:04:23,794 --> 00:04:25,296
It identifies the patterns in crimes

101
00:04:25,421 --> 00:04:28,090
and assesses whether or not they were
committed by the same perpetrator.

102
00:04:28,632 --> 00:04:30,759
Wow. Cool.

103
00:04:31,343 --> 00:04:32,845
The systems flagged your two murders

104
00:04:33,179 --> 00:04:36,140
as the re-emergence of a killer
known as "the Caveman."

105
00:04:36,807 --> 00:04:38,184
I remember him.

106
00:04:38,309 --> 00:04:40,227
He bludgeoned his victims
with a wooden club.

107
00:04:40,769 --> 00:04:42,146
That was ten years ago.

108
00:04:42,271 --> 00:04:44,523
Indeed, but the similarities
between his victims

109
00:04:44,648 --> 00:04:47,443
and the victims in this case
are statistically identical.

110
00:04:47,568 --> 00:04:48,944
How so?

111
00:04:49,069 --> 00:04:51,572
The Caveman always picks
two victims, one week apart.

112
00:04:51,780 --> 00:04:54,867
The same physical attributes...
brunettes, 120 to 140 pounds.

113
00:04:55,284 --> 00:04:57,828
In every other respect, the choice
seems entirely random...

114
00:04:58,245 --> 00:05:00,164
no geographic or demographic pattern.

115
00:05:00,706 --> 00:05:02,249
Victims are bludgeoned
on the side of the head

116
00:05:02,374 --> 00:05:04,960
with a wooden object,
no evidence of sexual assault

117
00:05:05,377 --> 00:05:07,546
bodies are left in waste sites
and scrap yards

118
00:05:07,671 --> 00:05:09,381
and the victims are unknown
to each other.

119
00:05:09,715 --> 00:05:11,342
This is classic Caveman.

120
00:05:12,635 --> 00:05:15,679
Well, at least you're wrong with conviction.
These two women knew each other.

121
00:05:16,222 --> 00:05:17,598
How do you know that?

122
00:05:18,182 --> 00:05:19,558
Okay, so this one...

123
00:05:20,226 --> 00:05:23,145
Yes, now if you look at the detail
in this rose

124
00:05:24,104 --> 00:05:28,442
and then again at the detail in this scarf
they were both done by the same hand.

125
00:05:29,610 --> 00:05:31,529
- Intriguing handicraft theory.
- Yeah.

126
00:05:32,029 --> 00:05:34,740
But even if we were able to establish a link
between the victims

127
00:05:35,115 --> 00:05:36,909
it doesn't exclude the Caveman
from being the killer.

128
00:05:37,535 --> 00:05:39,578
It merely expands the modeling paradigm.

129
00:05:39,787 --> 00:05:42,164
Merely expands the modeling paradigm.

130
00:05:42,289 --> 00:05:45,626
Wasn't the Caveman known
for sending poetry, animal organs

131
00:05:45,751 --> 00:05:47,336
even crude drawings to the police?

132
00:05:47,461 --> 00:05:48,879
We haven't received anything like that.

133
00:05:49,088 --> 00:05:50,464
- We will.
- Hey, boss.

134
00:05:50,881 --> 00:05:52,967
Found out that Valerie Bestin
was a financial director

135
00:05:53,092 --> 00:05:55,094
at an online company called Agile Turtle.

136
00:05:55,469 --> 00:05:57,721
Dr. Montague, this is agent Wayne Rigsby.

137
00:05:57,888 --> 00:06:01,183
Rigsby, Dr. Montague's consulting on the case
and you'll be her liaison.

138
00:06:01,308 --> 00:06:02,685
It's a pleasure to meet you.

139
00:06:02,810 --> 00:06:04,937
- Why don't you show her around?
- Sure, I can give you the 50-cent tour.

140
00:06:05,396 --> 00:06:06,772
Start in the major crimes section.

141
00:06:07,439 --> 00:06:10,609
Really? A decision science consultant?

142
00:06:10,734 --> 00:06:12,570
Don't start. This goes way above us.

143
00:06:12,695 --> 00:06:14,780
We're good to the feds,
the feds will be good to us.

144
00:06:15,114 --> 00:06:16,615
When in doubt, don't act out.

145
00:06:16,907 --> 00:06:18,659
You just made a rhyme.
When am I ever in doubt?

146
00:06:18,826 --> 00:06:21,328
- Seriously, you start trouble, and I will...
- You'll burst my bubble?

147
00:06:28,586 --> 00:06:29,962
She was such a good person.

148
00:06:32,881 --> 00:06:37,177
She took all these underprivileged-like kids
hiking at half dome earlier this year.

149
00:06:37,344 --> 00:06:39,847
Mr. Beechum, did Valerie
have any friends or co-workers

150
00:06:39,972 --> 00:06:42,057
- that were young Latinas?
- Mm, no.

151
00:06:43,183 --> 00:06:44,810
There's not really that many women
that work here.

152
00:06:45,394 --> 00:06:46,770
You had a crush on her, didn't you?

153
00:06:48,105 --> 00:06:50,149
- Why do you say that?
- She's an attractive young woman

154
00:06:51,191 --> 00:06:53,485
single computer
programmer, birds, bees.

155
00:06:54,361 --> 00:06:55,738
I could go on.

156
00:06:55,863 --> 00:06:57,406
I'm more of an entrepreneur
than a programmer these days

157
00:06:58,240 --> 00:07:01,368
but that isn't an untrue statement.
She was a lovely lady.

158
00:07:03,871 --> 00:07:05,581
But when you made your move,
she shot you down.

159
00:07:06,206 --> 00:07:08,208
No. Well...

160
00:07:09,251 --> 00:07:11,045
Yeah, I wouldn't really call it a move.

161
00:07:11,587 --> 00:07:12,963
But there were no hard feelings.

162
00:07:13,088 --> 00:07:15,591
I mean, if I killed every woman
that shot me down

163
00:07:15,716 --> 00:07:17,092
I would be a monster.

164
00:07:18,427 --> 00:07:21,347
You know, well...I mean, I'm not...
You know what I mean.

165
00:07:23,891 --> 00:07:28,312
- When was the last time you saw Valerie?
- Uh, yesterday at work.

166
00:07:28,937 --> 00:07:30,314
Okay, I think we're done here.

167
00:07:30,439 --> 00:07:31,815
- You do?
- Yes.

168
00:07:31,940 --> 00:07:33,317
Oh, actually, no.
I have one more question.

169
00:07:33,442 --> 00:07:34,818
Where's the nearest homeless shelter?

170
00:07:36,487 --> 00:07:39,448
Valerie was deeply guilt-ridden
over hitting the homeless guy.

171
00:07:39,657 --> 00:07:42,451
She started collecting soap,
shampoo, and crayons.

172
00:07:42,576 --> 00:07:43,952
She was taking kids hiking.

173
00:07:44,370 --> 00:07:46,664
- She probably volunteered her time...
- In a homeless shelter?

174
00:07:46,789 --> 00:07:48,749
Sometimes you act like I don't know Jack.

175
00:07:48,874 --> 00:07:50,876
More rhyming.
Now that's even rapping.

176
00:07:51,043 --> 00:07:53,420
Sometimes you act
like I don't know Jack.

177
00:07:54,046 --> 00:07:55,422
I'm-a drop a beat back there.

178
00:07:56,382 --> 00:07:57,758
Yeah.
Yee-yee.

179
00:07:58,300 --> 00:07:59,677
Valerie Bestin?

180
00:08:00,010 --> 00:08:01,970
Of course. She volunteered here.

181
00:08:02,805 --> 00:08:04,264
That's what I told the other people.

182
00:08:05,265 --> 00:08:08,060
- What other people?
- Um, those people.

183
00:08:11,188 --> 00:08:12,564
Hi.

184
00:08:12,731 --> 00:08:14,108
How'd you know to come here?

185
00:08:14,233 --> 00:08:16,610
Women are 17% more likely
than men to self-direct

186
00:08:16,735 --> 00:08:18,362
into redemptive recourse
after committing crimes.

187
00:08:18,904 --> 00:08:21,865
Given Valerie Bestin's lack of relapse
after D.U.I. with injury incident

188
00:08:21,990 --> 00:08:23,617
it only made sense
that she would follow the pattern

189
00:08:23,992 --> 00:08:25,577
so I called treatment facilities and shelters

190
00:08:25,703 --> 00:08:27,579
in surrounding areas
until I found the right place.

191
00:08:28,956 --> 00:08:30,416
So you... guessed.

192
00:08:30,791 --> 00:08:33,419
Ah, it's more of a scientific
hypothesis, really.

193
00:08:33,585 --> 00:08:35,504
- Just keep us in the loop.
- Noted.

194
00:08:36,046 --> 00:08:37,631
Need to head back
and code these interviews.

195
00:08:37,881 --> 00:08:39,258
If you'll excuse us.

196
00:08:42,344 --> 00:08:44,513
You have to admit,
she really knows her stuff.

197
00:08:44,638 --> 00:08:46,014
I'm admitting nothing.

198
00:08:46,974 --> 00:08:48,350
She's huffing and puffing.

199
00:08:49,226 --> 00:08:50,602
Wait. That rhymes.

200
00:08:51,645 --> 00:08:54,732
Like I told Dr. Montague,
I didn't know Valerie very well.

201
00:08:55,357 --> 00:08:56,900
But she would often bring
donations with her.

202
00:08:57,526 --> 00:08:58,902
Desperately needed.

203
00:08:59,069 --> 00:09:00,612
Ah, we're stretched pretty thin
around here

204
00:09:00,738 --> 00:09:02,114
so our focus is on our guests.

205
00:09:02,740 --> 00:09:05,534
Uh, but Paula had more contact
with her than I did.

206
00:09:06,034 --> 00:09:07,578
Valerie was a saint.

207
00:09:07,911 --> 00:09:09,496
She never talked down to nobody.

208
00:09:10,122 --> 00:09:13,250
Always makin' sure we was eatin' better
drinkin' less.

209
00:09:13,917 --> 00:09:15,461
Oh, when Marty died...

210
00:09:16,754 --> 00:09:19,465
She was, like... cryin'.

211
00:09:19,923 --> 00:09:21,300
She really cared.

212
00:09:21,425 --> 00:09:24,636
- Marty?
- A former guest.

213
00:09:24,887 --> 00:09:29,266
He passed away here a couple weeks ago.
A bad case of flu swept through here.

214
00:09:29,391 --> 00:09:32,561
Lot of people got sick,
and poor Marty's system just...

215
00:09:33,645 --> 00:09:35,230
Just couldn't handle it, you know?

216
00:09:38,609 --> 00:09:40,110
Any other questions, Jane?

217
00:09:40,402 --> 00:09:43,030
Uh, yes, um,
I do have one more question...

218
00:09:43,363 --> 00:09:44,740
- Yeah.
- In fact.

219
00:09:45,032 --> 00:09:48,243
Do either of you recall whether
Valerie was a-a knitter?

220
00:09:50,370 --> 00:09:52,331
- Knitting.
- Thank you for your time.

221
00:09:53,081 --> 00:09:54,458
All right. Thank you.

222
00:09:58,545 --> 00:10:00,088
Just got back the forensics report.

223
00:10:00,422 --> 00:10:02,549
Both victims were bludgeoned
with the same object.

224
00:10:02,758 --> 00:10:04,718
Identical wood fibers
were found in both their wounds.

225
00:10:05,302 --> 00:10:06,678
Sounds like the Caveman.

226
00:10:07,179 --> 00:10:09,848
I don't think he has the patent
on killing with a 2x4.

227
00:10:10,641 --> 00:10:12,601
You know, we should think
about a press response to this.

228
00:10:12,935 --> 00:10:15,896
Being ahead of media speculation
makes us look less reactive.

229
00:10:16,021 --> 00:10:17,981
Well, hang on.
We're not even sure it's the Caveman.

230
00:10:18,106 --> 00:10:19,650
We don't need to speak
to the press just yet.

231
00:10:19,775 --> 00:10:21,151
I disagree.

232
00:10:28,575 --> 00:10:32,287
But I hear you...
and I understand your concerns.

233
00:10:32,496 --> 00:10:34,414
Yes, you hear me, most certainly.

234
00:10:34,915 --> 00:10:37,167
But I doubt very much you understand
my concerns.

235
00:10:37,668 --> 00:10:39,419
If you're so sure
that it's not the Caveman

236
00:10:39,545 --> 00:10:40,921
why are you reading up on his file?

237
00:10:41,463 --> 00:10:43,882
I like to read.
I have insomnia.

238
00:10:44,925 --> 00:10:46,301
I don't like warm milk.

239
00:10:46,426 --> 00:10:47,803
I'm lactose-intolerant.
I could go on.

240
00:10:47,928 --> 00:10:51,014
Did you know that habitual mockery
is a strong indicator of depression?

241
00:10:52,891 --> 00:10:55,310
No. But thank you for enlightening me.

242
00:10:58,772 --> 00:11:00,941
Um, that was inappropriate.

243
00:11:02,109 --> 00:11:03,485
I apologize.

244
00:11:09,992 --> 00:11:11,869
Oh, this is the last address.

245
00:11:13,579 --> 00:11:15,831
One of these vans has to have been
at the salvage yard.

246
00:11:16,498 --> 00:11:19,042
- How many left?
- Nine.

247
00:11:19,459 --> 00:11:21,962
Oh, we already hit eight.
God, my feet hurt.

248
00:11:22,546 --> 00:11:23,922
Your feet hurt?

249
00:11:25,757 --> 00:11:27,134
Is there some way we can work this out?

250
00:11:28,260 --> 00:11:30,387
What? I have to spell it out?

251
00:11:31,847 --> 00:11:33,223
Okay, look, I lied.

252
00:11:33,348 --> 00:11:34,808
I lied to the P.S.U.
to protect my dad

253
00:11:34,933 --> 00:11:37,019
and you had to lie to cover me,
and I owe you big-time.

254
00:11:37,311 --> 00:11:38,729
Look, I get that you're mad at me,
and I'm sorry.

255
00:11:38,854 --> 00:11:40,230
But please, can we move past this?

256
00:11:41,690 --> 00:11:43,567
- Okay.
- Okay?

257
00:11:43,775 --> 00:11:45,402
What, like... like, we're okay, okay?

258
00:11:49,323 --> 00:11:50,699
Police!

259
00:11:51,241 --> 00:11:52,618
Hey, what the hell, man?

260
00:11:52,743 --> 00:11:54,411
CBI. We need to ask you
a couple questions.

261
00:11:55,370 --> 00:11:56,872
- You the owner of this van?
- Yeah.

262
00:11:58,123 --> 00:11:59,499
You get your transmission
fixed this morning?

263
00:12:00,167 --> 00:12:01,543
Yeah. Why?

264
00:12:01,877 --> 00:12:06,381
- Ma'am, where'd you get that bracelet?
- At the none-of-your-damn-business store.

265
00:12:10,177 --> 00:12:12,220
So did your friend Yasmeen
in the back of the van

266
00:12:12,346 --> 00:12:14,848
- know where this bracelet came from?
- Oh, that wasn't a friend.

267
00:12:15,599 --> 00:12:16,975
That was just a little dinner.

268
00:12:17,100 --> 00:12:18,560
It's weird that your dinner's name
was Yasmeen

269
00:12:19,311 --> 00:12:21,772
but this bracelet you gave
her has the initials "F.T." engraved on it.

270
00:12:22,189 --> 00:12:23,565
Imagine that.

271
00:12:23,690 --> 00:12:25,442
It's too bad you forgot the matching earrings
off the woman you killed.

272
00:12:26,568 --> 00:12:27,945
I didn't kill anybody.

273
00:12:28,904 --> 00:12:30,447
Then how'd you end up
with a dead woman's bracelet?

274
00:12:32,908 --> 00:12:35,202
You know, decomposing bodies
can still leave a lot of evidence?

275
00:12:36,370 --> 00:12:38,163
Only a matter of time before
they match up the D.N.A.

276
00:12:38,288 --> 00:12:39,665
Left on this bracelet to her body.

277
00:12:41,833 --> 00:12:45,128
Look... sometimes I haul junk
to that yard.

278
00:12:45,671 --> 00:12:47,422
I was there looking around
for whatever.

279
00:12:47,881 --> 00:12:50,175
There she was, already dead.

280
00:12:51,760 --> 00:12:53,136
The bracelet wasn't doing her any good.

281
00:12:55,222 --> 00:12:56,890
Too much of a wuss to be a killer.

282
00:12:57,015 --> 00:13:00,727
Lacking in grandiosity, only one divorce
no traffic violations.

283
00:13:02,020 --> 00:13:04,272
An undercurrent of rage would normally
express itself

284
00:13:04,398 --> 00:13:05,774
at least in a driving record.

285
00:13:06,483 --> 00:13:08,193
I'm not seeing a propensity
for violence here.

286
00:13:08,318 --> 00:13:10,862
- So you agree with me?
- Not our killer.

287
00:13:13,740 --> 00:13:15,117
Thank you.

288
00:13:16,952 --> 00:13:19,538
The Caveman was never known
to steal jewelry off his victims

289
00:13:20,038 --> 00:13:21,540
so that clears up that anomaly.

290
00:13:21,915 --> 00:13:24,710
Life's just one giant mathematical
equation for you, isn't it?

291
00:13:24,960 --> 00:13:26,670
I would like that. I confess.

292
00:13:26,795 --> 00:13:28,964
- This isn't the Caveman.
- So you say.

293
00:13:29,589 --> 00:13:31,091
I got $1 that says the same thing.

294
00:13:32,801 --> 00:13:35,262
I have no objection to gambling.
You're on.

295
00:13:40,100 --> 00:13:42,227
- Oh, someone's popular.
- Hardly.

296
00:13:43,395 --> 00:13:44,771
What is it?

297
00:13:45,022 --> 00:13:46,398
What the hell?

298
00:13:46,857 --> 00:13:48,233
Looks like a kidney.

299
00:13:48,817 --> 00:13:50,944
There's a poem on the other side
of that paper, I suspect?

300
00:13:51,361 --> 00:13:52,738
You've seen this before?

301
00:13:53,363 --> 00:13:55,615
"The ladies are dead.
I crushed their heads."

302
00:13:55,782 --> 00:13:58,243
"And after the lash,
they go into the trash."

303
00:13:58,660 --> 00:14:00,037
It's from the Caveman.

304
00:14:00,162 --> 00:14:02,247
Poetry week. You could've done
better than that.

305
00:14:02,956 --> 00:14:05,709
- I believe we had a wager.
- Ah. Yeah.

306
00:14:07,544 --> 00:14:08,920
Thank you.

307
00:14:14,132 --> 00:14:15,925
"We've received reliable notification"

308
00:14:16,051 --> 00:14:18,553
"from an individual calling
himself the Caveman"

309
00:14:19,220 --> 00:14:22,182
"who claims responsibility for the murder
of two local area women..."

310
00:14:22,390 --> 00:14:24,934
- Hey.
- Ms. Montague in her element.

311
00:14:25,477 --> 00:14:28,730
It's not often you see a profiler
basking in the spotlight.

312
00:14:29,731 --> 00:14:31,441
Well, Montague says
that a killer like the Caveman

313
00:14:31,691 --> 00:14:33,151
craves acknowledgment for his crimes.

314
00:14:33,526 --> 00:14:36,237
Giving him some attention could keep him
from taking another victim early.

315
00:14:38,156 --> 00:14:39,991
- You like Montague, don't you?
No.

316
00:14:40,700 --> 00:14:43,244
- I mean, I... don't dislike her.
- What's to dislike?

317
00:14:43,870 --> 00:14:45,580
Right. Still working on, uh...

318
00:14:45,705 --> 00:14:47,082
where the Caveman
sent the package from

319
00:14:47,207 --> 00:14:49,042
but we got an I.D.
on the other victim.

320
00:14:49,375 --> 00:14:51,211
Excellent. Fernanda Talan.

321
00:14:51,503 --> 00:14:53,296
Engraved initials on the back
of her stolen bracelet

322
00:14:53,421 --> 00:14:54,798
"helped make a positive match."

323
00:14:54,923 --> 00:14:56,883
"Her cousins reported her missing
about a week ago."

324
00:14:57,092 --> 00:14:59,969
"She worked for them.
Dos Hermanos bottling plant."

325
00:15:05,809 --> 00:15:08,186
Still wearing that brooch, I see
but where's the power suit?

326
00:15:08,645 --> 00:15:10,939
Well, wearing a palette of neutral tones
is less threatening

327
00:15:11,064 --> 00:15:12,440
when interviewing people.

328
00:15:12,857 --> 00:15:14,234
- Hi.
- "Hey, boss."

329
00:15:14,359 --> 00:15:16,402
I just got Valerie Bestin's phone records.

330
00:15:16,528 --> 00:15:19,072
Lately she's been getting a lot of calls
from Donovan Treadlow

331
00:15:19,489 --> 00:15:20,865
the homeless shelter director.

332
00:15:21,658 --> 00:15:23,952
- He said he barely knew her.
- "Well, he lied.'

333
00:15:24,786 --> 00:15:26,246
Take Cho. Get the story.

334
00:15:27,789 --> 00:15:30,041
Fernanda was our cousin
from my mother's family.

335
00:15:30,542 --> 00:15:33,336
She came to us to get away
from the violence in Oakland.

336
00:15:34,129 --> 00:15:36,673
This wasn't supposed to happen to her.

337
00:15:38,133 --> 00:15:40,844
Her brother was killed just last year,
and now this?

338
00:15:42,637 --> 00:15:44,013
She was going to make it.

339
00:15:45,265 --> 00:15:46,641
Calm down, Nilda.

340
00:15:47,225 --> 00:15:48,893
When was the last time
you saw Fernanda?

341
00:15:49,102 --> 00:15:50,478
About a week ago.

342
00:15:50,603 --> 00:15:52,939
She took the bus after work
and just vanished.

343
00:15:53,606 --> 00:15:55,984
We were worried immediately
when she didn't show up the next day

344
00:15:56,109 --> 00:15:57,694
because she never missed work.

345
00:15:57,944 --> 00:15:59,320
Where did she work?

346
00:15:59,445 --> 00:16:00,822
- On the line.
- In there?

347
00:16:02,073 --> 00:16:04,075
Have you seen Valerie Bestin
around your neighborhood?

348
00:16:04,325 --> 00:16:05,702
No.

349
00:16:05,827 --> 00:16:07,579
We only heard about her
through the news and you all.

350
00:16:08,830 --> 00:16:10,665
I like the packaging.
What flavor is that?

351
00:16:11,207 --> 00:16:15,336
Um... probably our orange cream sodas,
right, Esther?

352
00:16:16,254 --> 00:16:20,967
Yes. We make natural sodas.
No corn syrup, no artificial flavors.

353
00:16:21,176 --> 00:16:23,136
We employ locals, pay them right.

354
00:16:23,845 --> 00:16:26,139
People used to shake their heads
when we first started

355
00:16:26,264 --> 00:16:27,807
but now they're getting it.

356
00:16:28,349 --> 00:16:30,393
Fernanda had made
a huge change here.

357
00:16:31,394 --> 00:16:36,149
She wanted to be a nurse and...
It's just all so sad.

358
00:16:38,067 --> 00:16:39,903
Is this a boy or girl?
I can't really see.

359
00:16:40,695 --> 00:16:42,071
It's a boy.

360
00:16:42,197 --> 00:16:43,573
A boy lizard.
What's his name?

361
00:16:43,990 --> 00:16:45,950
- Chuy.
- Hey, Chuy.

362
00:16:46,451 --> 00:16:49,579
Ms. Montague, you're awfully quiet.
I'm sure you have some questions.

363
00:16:50,580 --> 00:16:53,333
Ms. Montague here...
she's, uh, super, super clever.

364
00:16:53,458 --> 00:16:55,793
- She's a scientist.
- Thank you, Mr. Jane.

365
00:16:56,044 --> 00:16:57,420
Oh, pleasure.

366
00:16:57,587 --> 00:17:00,882
- Did Fernanda ever abuse alcohol?
- No.

367
00:17:01,549 --> 00:17:03,760
What about illegal
or controlled substances?

368
00:17:04,344 --> 00:17:07,639
What are you trying to say
that Fernanda got mixed up in drugs?

369
00:17:07,889 --> 00:17:09,807
I'm looking into traits
and habits which might give

370
00:17:09,933 --> 00:17:12,060
some insight
into why she was chosen by the Caveman.

371
00:17:12,727 --> 00:17:15,647
Finding out why Fernanda's to blame
for getting murdered?

372
00:17:16,481 --> 00:17:17,982
What kind of quack are you?

373
00:17:19,275 --> 00:17:21,611
I'm sure that's not
what Dr. Montague meant.

374
00:17:21,736 --> 00:17:23,821
Uh, it appears that you've got this covered

375
00:17:23,947 --> 00:17:26,407
so I'm just going to, uh,
go inside and take a look around.

376
00:17:26,658 --> 00:17:28,493
Ma'am, I did not mean to offend.

377
00:17:29,202 --> 00:17:31,621
If you let me explain
my procedure to you...

378
00:17:41,172 --> 00:17:43,716
Fernanda was a sweetheart,
hard worker.

379
00:17:44,467 --> 00:17:46,552
She had gotten pretty sick
a couple of weeks back

380
00:17:46,678 --> 00:17:50,098
- but there she was, on the job.
- Sick how?

381
00:17:50,890 --> 00:17:52,350
Stomach bug, something.

382
00:17:53,017 --> 00:17:55,353
- Dedicated.
- Absolutely.

383
00:17:56,104 --> 00:17:58,940
Shy at first, but then
she really got into it

384
00:17:59,232 --> 00:18:02,610
asking how we did things, you know
just learning the process.

385
00:18:03,695 --> 00:18:05,697
Can I, um, try one of these sodas?

386
00:18:06,572 --> 00:18:07,949
Absolutely.

387
00:18:11,077 --> 00:18:12,453
Cheers.

388
00:18:15,123 --> 00:18:16,499
Yeah, that's good.

389
00:18:16,874 --> 00:18:18,793
What's, uh, what's happening
tomorrow night?

390
00:18:20,878 --> 00:18:23,298
They're having a candlelight vigil
for Fernanda.

391
00:18:25,800 --> 00:18:28,386
Is the Helios Street Line
difficult to get to?

392
00:18:28,970 --> 00:18:30,471
No. Bus stop is right out front.

393
00:18:37,061 --> 00:18:38,438
Hey!

394
00:18:38,563 --> 00:18:40,231
Hey, uh, Lisbon's just finishing up
in there.

395
00:18:40,523 --> 00:18:41,899
Won't be a moment.

396
00:18:42,942 --> 00:18:44,902
- What'd you find out?
- Various things.

397
00:18:45,403 --> 00:18:48,072
- All data is useful data.
- So nothing then?

398
00:18:48,740 --> 00:18:50,116
And you?

399
00:18:50,241 --> 00:18:52,869
Well, I found out how our two victims
were connected.

400
00:18:54,078 --> 00:18:56,289
- How?
- Get on the bus. I'll show you.

401
00:18:57,165 --> 00:18:58,541
Wait. But my car is...

402
00:18:58,666 --> 00:19:00,043
Nah, just get on the bus.
Come on.

403
00:19:06,174 --> 00:19:08,718
Mr. Treadlow, you had a late night
conversation

404
00:19:08,843 --> 00:19:10,845
with Valerie Bestin the day
before she died.

405
00:19:10,970 --> 00:19:12,347
You say you hardly knew her

406
00:19:12,472 --> 00:19:14,640
and there you are calling her at midnight.
Why'd you lie?

407
00:19:17,018 --> 00:19:21,314
How it looks is bad
but it was shelter business.

408
00:19:21,481 --> 00:19:22,857
Your wife can confirm that.

409
00:19:23,649 --> 00:19:25,860
- That's not necessary.
- Then tell us about those calls.

410
00:19:28,863 --> 00:19:32,909
Valerie was always thinking she saw me
being too friendly with our female guests.

411
00:19:33,409 --> 00:19:36,079
- What did she see?
- Me comforting them.

412
00:19:37,080 --> 00:19:39,290
Never discount the power of healing touch.

413
00:19:39,832 --> 00:19:41,876
So how often are you healing
the homeless women around here?

414
00:19:42,627 --> 00:19:44,003
Don't make it sound so sordid.

415
00:19:44,629 --> 00:19:46,464
If there were nothing wrong,
your wife would be standing here.

416
00:19:48,591 --> 00:19:49,967
Look...

417
00:19:50,343 --> 00:19:53,137
Valerie kept yammering about
how she was gonna tell my wife if I didn't.

418
00:19:53,346 --> 00:19:57,809
So I was trying to persuade her
to let me handle it in my own way.

419
00:19:58,393 --> 00:19:59,894
We've looked into you, Mr. Treadlow.

420
00:20:00,478 --> 00:20:02,730
You and your wife started
running this shelter nine months ago?

421
00:20:03,648 --> 00:20:05,108
Yes, when we moved to the area.

422
00:20:05,483 --> 00:20:07,235
But this isn't the first time
you lived around here.

423
00:20:08,027 --> 00:20:10,363
You worked construction here.
Ten years ago, right?

424
00:20:11,406 --> 00:20:13,699
I did. What are you getting at?

425
00:20:13,866 --> 00:20:15,576
Well, you left town
in the middle of a housing boom

426
00:20:15,910 --> 00:20:17,703
just after a serial killer
named "the Caveman"

427
00:20:17,829 --> 00:20:19,205
stopped murdering the local women.

428
00:20:19,372 --> 00:20:21,416
And now you're back,
and here's the Caveman again.

429
00:20:21,707 --> 00:20:23,084
Crazy, right?

430
00:20:23,835 --> 00:20:25,211
Come on.

431
00:20:25,336 --> 00:20:26,712
You need to come with us.

432
00:20:27,296 --> 00:20:28,673
Please.

433
00:20:39,475 --> 00:20:40,852
Now that, uh...

434
00:20:41,436 --> 00:20:42,979
Brooch that you wear all the time...

435
00:20:43,938 --> 00:20:47,024
Does it carry any special
meaning or significance?

436
00:20:47,150 --> 00:20:48,526
- No.
- No?

437
00:20:48,901 --> 00:20:52,363
I only own one piece of jewelry.
Saves time and mental energy.

438
00:20:52,947 --> 00:20:54,323
That's very efficient.

439
00:20:55,700 --> 00:20:58,744
- Lucky it looks very good on you.
- Thank you.

440
00:21:07,879 --> 00:21:09,255
That's Agile Turtle.

441
00:21:10,214 --> 00:21:11,674
So the office where Valerie Bestin worked

442
00:21:11,924 --> 00:21:15,178
is on the same bus route as the factory
where Fernanda Talan worked.

443
00:21:16,971 --> 00:21:18,764
The homeless
shelter's on the same line, too.

444
00:21:19,307 --> 00:21:22,268
All right, that's... that's a very
suggestive link, but...

445
00:21:22,685 --> 00:21:25,938
Lisbon? Yes, I ditched you.
Uh, I must confess.

446
00:21:26,063 --> 00:21:28,065
But look at the bright side.
I left you with a car this time.

447
00:21:29,525 --> 00:21:31,194
There's no need to be so grumpy.
It's okay.

448
00:21:31,527 --> 00:21:35,198
I call bearing news.
Our victims met on the Helios line.

449
00:21:36,324 --> 00:21:37,700
It's a bus route.

450
00:21:38,451 --> 00:21:39,827
A bus.

451
00:21:40,203 --> 00:21:41,787
Public transportation.
Yes.

452
00:21:42,455 --> 00:21:46,250
And I would bet that the killer
met the victims on this same bus.

453
00:21:49,754 --> 00:21:51,964
Well... it's always a pleasure.

454
00:21:53,132 --> 00:21:54,509
Okay. Bye-bye.

455
00:21:54,800 --> 00:21:56,844
- Pure surmise.
- Yes.

456
00:21:58,012 --> 00:21:59,388
Pure.

457
00:22:00,932 --> 00:22:02,350
Anything on the Jane bus angle?

458
00:22:02,517 --> 00:22:04,268
I found out Valerie Bestin
barely used her car

459
00:22:04,393 --> 00:22:05,937
even though she got her license back
a year ago.

460
00:22:06,062 --> 00:22:07,563
She mainly took the bus,
just like Fernanda.

461
00:22:09,357 --> 00:22:11,692
- Cho, I thought you were with Treadlow.
- We're letting him think a while.

462
00:22:13,402 --> 00:22:14,779
I'm working with the D.A.'s office

463
00:22:14,904 --> 00:22:16,280
to get all the personnel files
released by the bus company.

464
00:22:16,405 --> 00:22:17,782
Van Pelt?

465
00:22:17,907 --> 00:22:21,077
I'm running filters on all the riders
with bus passes to see if anything flags.

466
00:22:21,410 --> 00:22:22,787
Nothing as of yet.

467
00:22:22,912 --> 00:22:25,331
Okay, and when you're done with that,
find my old couch.

468
00:22:41,347 --> 00:22:43,432
- There you go.
- Thanks.

469
00:22:53,859 --> 00:22:55,861
I don't recall
there being a strong correlation

470
00:22:55,987 --> 00:22:58,364
between the victims
and the use of public transportation.

471
00:22:59,657 --> 00:23:01,909
The Caveman's adapting.
I'll need to update my model.

472
00:23:02,285 --> 00:23:04,370
- Ms. Montague, just...
- Doctor. Dr. Montague.

473
00:23:04,495 --> 00:23:06,581
- Dr. Ms. Montague.
- Just doctor.

474
00:23:06,747 --> 00:23:08,124
- Doctor?
- Yes.

475
00:23:08,249 --> 00:23:09,625
Montague.

476
00:23:10,376 --> 00:23:13,045
Turn the super computer off.
Sit back, relax, and enjoy the ride...

477
00:23:13,170 --> 00:23:14,797
And the company, if possible.

478
00:23:15,715 --> 00:23:17,508
- Mr. Jane.
- Yes. Got it in one.

479
00:23:17,633 --> 00:23:19,760
Yes. On a notion...

480
00:23:20,303 --> 00:23:22,346
You have us sitting on a bus
because our victims

481
00:23:22,555 --> 00:23:24,432
might have been on the bus
at the same time?

482
00:23:24,557 --> 00:23:26,809
This is... messy.

483
00:23:27,560 --> 00:23:30,896
Can I ask you a question
as professionals?

484
00:23:31,022 --> 00:23:32,982
Two professionals... peers, if you will.

485
00:23:33,899 --> 00:23:39,405
Why can't a notion, a thought, or an idea
just hang out there for a moment?

486
00:23:39,655 --> 00:23:41,032
Just hang.

487
00:23:41,157 --> 00:23:42,617
Because life is order and sequence.

488
00:23:43,534 --> 00:23:44,952
Death is disorder, entropy.

489
00:23:46,287 --> 00:23:47,663
Did you know that California

490
00:23:47,788 --> 00:23:49,457
leads the nation in the number
of serial killers?

491
00:23:50,291 --> 00:23:52,335
Top security, at least.
Excuse me.

492
00:23:53,669 --> 00:23:55,046
Pardon me, ma'am.

493
00:23:55,171 --> 00:23:59,008
Uh, I couldn't help but notice
the beautiful detail work in this scarf.

494
00:23:59,133 --> 00:24:01,427
- Did you knit this yourself?
- Yes, I did.

495
00:24:01,677 --> 00:24:03,512
That's very nice.
You know, you could sell these.

496
00:24:03,638 --> 00:24:06,223
Do you... do you...
Have you thought of selling these?

497
00:24:06,349 --> 00:24:08,225
Yeah.
You... you want to look?

498
00:24:08,351 --> 00:24:10,770
If you don't mind.
They're very nice.

499
00:24:11,520 --> 00:24:13,731
She's got some great stuff here.
Beautiful.

500
00:24:14,649 --> 00:24:17,777
Can I just try this on my...
Lady friend?

501
00:24:20,821 --> 00:24:23,157
Look at this. Look at that.
It's a very similar style...

502
00:24:23,282 --> 00:24:25,159
- Stop.
- To the one that Fernanda was wearing

503
00:24:25,284 --> 00:24:28,079
and it looks just terrific
with her ensemble, no?

504
00:24:28,287 --> 00:24:29,664
Yes.

505
00:24:29,789 --> 00:24:32,500
Excuse me, ma'am. Hello.
We're investigating the murder...

506
00:24:32,958 --> 00:24:35,378
I'm so sorry. No understand.

507
00:24:35,503 --> 00:24:37,380
- If I could just ask you...
- No understand.

508
00:24:37,505 --> 00:24:39,256
But if you could
just wait a second, I...

509
00:24:39,882 --> 00:24:41,258
Excellent people skills.

510
00:24:44,011 --> 00:24:45,388
That's sarcasm.

511
00:24:47,056 --> 00:24:48,432
We can work on that.

512
00:24:53,354 --> 00:24:54,730
- Hey.
- Hey.

513
00:24:54,855 --> 00:24:56,816
I was going through the personnel files
for the bus drivers

514
00:24:56,982 --> 00:24:59,068
and something flagged.

515
00:24:59,276 --> 00:25:01,904
A bus driver, his name
is Ralph Mercer.

516
00:25:02,238 --> 00:25:04,573
He's only been with the bus company
for the last six months.

517
00:25:05,199 --> 00:25:06,575
So?

518
00:25:06,701 --> 00:25:09,453
According to his social security number,
he's been dead 15 years.

519
00:25:09,995 --> 00:25:11,664
So I did a little more digging

520
00:25:11,831 --> 00:25:15,251
and fingerprint records say
Mercer is actually Arthur Coffey.

521
00:25:15,835 --> 00:25:18,754
Not a nice guy... long sheet,
including aggravated rape

522
00:25:18,879 --> 00:25:20,256
and assault with a deadly weapon.

523
00:25:20,423 --> 00:25:22,091
He drives the same route
our victims rode?

524
00:25:22,550 --> 00:25:25,886
- Afternoon and evening shifts.
- Great. Where is he now?

525
00:25:26,470 --> 00:25:28,639
Got his route schedule from his boss.
He's on the road now.

526
00:25:33,227 --> 00:25:34,603
Hello, everybody.
I'm Patrick.

527
00:25:34,895 --> 00:25:36,272
Uh, I'm sure you've all heard

528
00:25:36,397 --> 00:25:38,941
about the two local women
found murdered. Yes?

529
00:25:40,651 --> 00:25:42,027
Yes.

530
00:25:42,153 --> 00:25:45,823
Fernanda Talan and Valerie Bestin...
They rode this bus every day.

531
00:25:46,782 --> 00:25:48,993
Yes, they did, and we...
when I say "we"

532
00:25:49,118 --> 00:25:53,664
I include my charming associate,
Dr. Montague.

533
00:25:54,373 --> 00:25:56,375
We wanna know
what you know about them...

534
00:25:56,917 --> 00:26:01,255
And any other shady people
that you may have noticed on the bus.

535
00:26:02,214 --> 00:26:03,674
Any information may be important.

536
00:26:05,342 --> 00:26:10,556
Any little piece of information may...
be important.

537
00:26:15,311 --> 00:26:16,687
Anything at all?

538
00:26:17,354 --> 00:26:18,731
Any detail?

539
00:26:19,732 --> 00:26:21,108
Okay. Thank you.

540
00:26:28,532 --> 00:26:29,909
Excellent people skills.

541
00:26:31,327 --> 00:26:33,829
Ah, sarcasm. That's very good.
You're learning.

542
00:26:34,622 --> 00:26:38,042
But, uh, I, in fact, got
an excellent response.

543
00:26:38,167 --> 00:26:40,336
I'm told that the driver is a little shady.

544
00:26:40,711 --> 00:26:42,254
- Really?
- Yes, really.

545
00:26:44,340 --> 00:26:45,758
What the hell is goin' on? Huh?

546
00:26:47,968 --> 00:26:49,345
How did you...

547
00:27:03,150 --> 00:27:04,527
Why are you guys following me?

548
00:27:04,652 --> 00:27:06,320
Uh, we'd like to ask you
some questions.

549
00:27:06,737 --> 00:27:08,113
Are you cops, or...

550
00:27:08,239 --> 00:27:10,074
No, we're consultants
with law enforcement.

551
00:27:13,077 --> 00:27:15,871
Actually, no, she's with law enforcement.
I'm just an innocent bystander.

552
00:27:15,996 --> 00:27:18,374
- She lured me into this whole mess.
- He's getting away.

553
00:27:18,666 --> 00:27:20,042
- Do, uh, do something.
- That's good.

554
00:27:20,167 --> 00:27:21,544
Like what? What am I gonna do?

555
00:27:22,920 --> 00:27:24,296
Drop the weapon!

556
00:27:24,505 --> 00:27:25,881
Facedown to the ground now!

557
00:27:27,550 --> 00:27:28,926
That's nice timing.

558
00:27:30,344 --> 00:27:33,097
Darn! With him in the back,
there won't be room for you two.

559
00:27:34,265 --> 00:27:36,767
- Well, how are we gonna get back?
- Take the bus.

560
00:27:36,892 --> 00:27:38,269
Come on, get up.

561
00:27:40,521 --> 00:27:41,897
He was our driver.

562
00:27:47,376 --> 00:27:50,963
Nice sheet, Arthur...
carjacking, extortion, rape.

563
00:27:51,630 --> 00:27:53,799
Now that we've found 30 bindles
on you, we can add dealing as well.

564
00:27:55,050 --> 00:27:57,219
I wanna straighten out, okay?

565
00:27:57,344 --> 00:27:59,888
I got a disease... called addiction.

566
00:28:01,223 --> 00:28:02,599
That's all it is... the dealing.

567
00:28:02,891 --> 00:28:06,853
I'm just trying to, you know, maintain.

568
00:28:08,772 --> 00:28:10,899
I'm a good person that made
some wrong choices.

569
00:28:11,608 --> 00:28:13,568
Are you the firstborn in your family,
Mr. Coffey?

570
00:28:13,944 --> 00:28:15,320
Yeah.

571
00:28:15,445 --> 00:28:16,822
Ever heard of the Caveman?

572
00:28:17,030 --> 00:28:19,741
Sure. From the... TV news.

573
00:28:20,367 --> 00:28:21,743
You know these women?

574
00:28:22,369 --> 00:28:23,745
Yeah.

575
00:28:24,871 --> 00:28:27,582
They rode the bus.
They were killed by the Caveman.

576
00:28:28,125 --> 00:28:29,501
I mean, it was just on TV.

577
00:28:31,128 --> 00:28:34,965
In fact, yeah, it was you that was on TV
said it was the Caveman, right?

578
00:28:35,257 --> 00:28:36,633
Yeah.

579
00:28:38,969 --> 00:28:40,554
How many sexual partners
have you had?

580
00:28:40,679 --> 00:28:43,557
Wow. You like to cut to the chase.

581
00:28:44,933 --> 00:28:47,853
Um... like 100, 150.

582
00:28:48,770 --> 00:28:50,147
Women? Men?

583
00:28:50,647 --> 00:28:54,192
Hello. Women.
What is your point here?

584
00:28:54,359 --> 00:28:55,736
We need to know
whether you killed these women

585
00:28:55,861 --> 00:28:57,654
because they found out
about your side business

586
00:28:57,821 --> 00:28:59,197
or because you're the Caveman.

587
00:28:59,322 --> 00:29:02,659
Hey. No.
You think I'm the Caveman?

588
00:29:03,201 --> 00:29:04,578
No, no. You got me all wrong here.

589
00:29:04,703 --> 00:29:07,539
I deal a little blow, okay?
I don't do murders.

590
00:29:12,252 --> 00:29:13,628
I think I'll take a lawyer now.

591
00:29:16,339 --> 00:29:18,633
Well, he's the only suspect we have
with a direct link to both victims.

592
00:29:19,384 --> 00:29:21,887
Yes, it's all very compelling,
but I'm not convinced.

593
00:29:24,222 --> 00:29:26,016
And, yes, you should.

594
00:29:27,100 --> 00:29:28,977
- Should what?
- Oh, you know what.

595
00:29:29,936 --> 00:29:31,313
- No.
- Oh, yes, you do.

596
00:29:31,605 --> 00:29:33,231
- No, I don't.
- Yes, you do.

597
00:29:38,737 --> 00:29:41,573
That bus driver's an ass,
but he isn't the Caveman.

598
00:29:41,698 --> 00:29:43,075
I'm thinking the same thing.

599
00:29:43,200 --> 00:29:45,243
His shifts at work don't match up
with our timeline.

600
00:29:45,869 --> 00:29:48,747
And without any hard evidence,
I had to cut Treadlow loose

601
00:29:48,872 --> 00:29:50,248
so we have to start from scratch.

602
00:29:50,499 --> 00:29:53,293
- Damn it to hell!
- Oh, language. Please.

603
00:29:53,418 --> 00:29:55,879
Bertram is on Hightower's back.
She's on mine.

604
00:29:56,004 --> 00:29:58,173
It's all, "catch the Caveman,
catch the Caveman."

605
00:29:58,298 --> 00:29:59,674
I hate serial killers.

606
00:30:01,009 --> 00:30:04,137
Caveman didn't kill Fernanda
and Valerie. Trust me.

607
00:30:04,346 --> 00:30:05,722
Well, how can you be so sure?

608
00:30:07,099 --> 00:30:08,934
Because I wrote the letter
from the Caveman.

609
00:30:17,150 --> 00:30:20,070
What did you just say?
I wrote the letter from the Caveman.

610
00:30:20,195 --> 00:30:22,531
- Yeah, I heard you the first time.
- Why'd you ask me to say it again?

611
00:30:22,656 --> 00:30:25,575
- It was a rhetorical question!
- I just wanted to stir things up

612
00:30:25,700 --> 00:30:27,702
just, you know, give Montague
a little prod.

613
00:30:27,869 --> 00:30:30,705
- I get it. She irks you.
- No, she doesn't irk me. I like her.

614
00:30:30,831 --> 00:30:32,207
In fact, she amuses me.

615
00:30:32,332 --> 00:30:34,251
Yeah, and in the meantime,
everybody is freaking out

616
00:30:34,376 --> 00:30:36,128
because they think
there's a serial killer on the loose.

617
00:30:36,419 --> 00:30:39,840
Well, there is that, but...
that was her story.

618
00:30:39,965 --> 00:30:43,593
- She made that up, not me.
- Undoing this is gonna be a nightmare.

619
00:30:44,344 --> 00:30:47,389
The press is gonna have a field day.
I have to tell the families.

620
00:30:47,806 --> 00:30:50,642
Isn't there a candlelight vigil
somewhere this evening?

621
00:30:50,851 --> 00:30:52,227
Well, I think so.

622
00:30:53,895 --> 00:30:55,522
It's all gonna work out just fine.

623
00:30:56,189 --> 00:30:58,150
I have... no doubt in my mind.

624
00:31:05,532 --> 00:31:06,908
- Dr. Montague.
- Agent Rigsby.

625
00:31:10,453 --> 00:31:12,581
Interesting line of questioning
you had going in interrogation.

626
00:31:13,623 --> 00:31:15,333
Oh, well, yes, as you know

627
00:31:15,458 --> 00:31:19,296
sexual promiscuity and risky behavior
are reliable indicators in psychopathy.

628
00:31:19,462 --> 00:31:22,382
Uh, yes, you can learn a lot about a person
from their sexual history.

629
00:31:23,008 --> 00:31:25,510
I remember there was this one time
we had this guy in custody, and he had a...

630
00:31:26,761 --> 00:31:28,138
It was a...

631
00:31:28,263 --> 00:31:31,016
It's an inappropriate story,
sexual history-wise.

632
00:31:34,019 --> 00:31:35,812
This has been, uh, nice.

633
00:31:36,396 --> 00:31:38,273
- What has been nice?
- Talking with you.

634
00:31:38,857 --> 00:31:40,483
Oh, well. You're welcome.

635
00:31:42,152 --> 00:31:43,528
- Agent Rigsby.
- Yes?

636
00:31:43,653 --> 00:31:46,406
- Your tea.
- Yes. I...

637
00:31:47,657 --> 00:31:49,034
Will take that to go.

638
00:31:51,036 --> 00:31:53,079
What, uh, agent Rigsby wanted to say...

639
00:31:54,206 --> 00:31:56,458
Well, he... he wanted to ask you out
on a date tonight.

640
00:32:01,504 --> 00:32:03,965
I was... going to go
to the candlelight vigil...

641
00:32:04,090 --> 00:32:05,967
Oh, I got...
See, I picked that thing up. Um...

642
00:32:06,718 --> 00:32:10,555
- What? What have you got now?
- This is for you.

643
00:32:11,640 --> 00:32:13,016
Rigsby picked it out.

644
00:32:13,225 --> 00:32:16,353
I didn't. I have no idea what's in that box.
You didn't...

645
00:32:16,770 --> 00:32:18,146
- Yes, he did.
- No, I didn't.

646
00:32:25,570 --> 00:32:26,947
Thanks for coming out.

647
00:32:27,197 --> 00:32:30,242
Um, Fernanda's family was kind enough
to include us in this vigil

648
00:32:30,367 --> 00:32:32,744
because we're all working
toward the same end.

649
00:32:34,120 --> 00:32:37,749
We need to speak up.
We can't let a killer like the Caveman

650
00:32:37,874 --> 00:32:39,668
hold our community hostage.

651
00:32:40,543 --> 00:32:43,880
Don't let Fernanda and Valerie
die in vain.

652
00:32:44,381 --> 00:32:47,092
Uh, if you have something to say,
please come forward.

653
00:32:49,928 --> 00:32:51,304
Good evening.

654
00:32:51,429 --> 00:32:53,890
Uh, excuse me.
My name is Patrick Jane.

655
00:32:54,391 --> 00:32:55,850
Uh, I offer my condolences.

656
00:32:56,059 --> 00:32:58,603
I'm a part of the team
that will bring this killer to justice.

657
00:32:58,812 --> 00:33:00,480
I do have some unfortunate news.

658
00:33:01,523 --> 00:33:03,858
We've just learned
that the letter we believed

659
00:33:04,025 --> 00:33:07,654
to be from the Caveman is,
in fact, a very clever forgery.

660
00:33:08,280 --> 00:33:09,864
- So what does that mean?
- Bottom line...

661
00:33:09,990 --> 00:33:12,367
We're pretty sure that the Caveman
didn't kill your loved ones.

662
00:33:12,617 --> 00:33:15,287
Someone is using the Caveman as a foil.

663
00:33:15,453 --> 00:33:16,830
You all said it was him on TV.

664
00:33:17,038 --> 00:33:20,292
The profiler lady said
you were making good progress on the case.

665
00:33:20,417 --> 00:33:23,753
She made a mistake.
I'm sorry, but it happens.

666
00:33:23,878 --> 00:33:25,422
So do you know anything?

667
00:33:25,797 --> 00:33:28,842
Again, I'd like to apologize
profusely for our error.

668
00:33:29,050 --> 00:33:33,847
Uh... it appears that this,
uh, forger is, uh...

669
00:33:34,014 --> 00:33:35,557
one of unparalleled skill.

670
00:33:35,932 --> 00:33:39,102
Oh! Oh, my God.
That's blood.

671
00:33:42,522 --> 00:33:44,858
Oh. What is that?

672
00:33:46,026 --> 00:33:49,863
- The profiler lady was wearing that brooch.
- Yes, she was.

673
00:33:52,866 --> 00:33:54,826
I'm not a specialist,
but that looks like a kidney.

674
00:33:56,703 --> 00:33:58,079
There's a note.

675
00:33:58,371 --> 00:33:59,748
"Your profiler is dead."

676
00:33:59,873 --> 00:34:02,167
"Stupid copycat
will join her at midnight."

677
00:34:02,334 --> 00:34:04,210
Signed from the Caveman.

678
00:34:05,086 --> 00:34:08,590
Oh, no.
Lisbon, where are you?

679
00:34:09,883 --> 00:34:11,259
I need your help.

680
00:34:17,522 --> 00:34:18,940
Listen, thanks for tonight.
I had fun.

681
00:34:19,707 --> 00:34:21,084
So did I, Wayne.

682
00:34:21,459 --> 00:34:24,212
Um, I enjoyed your funny
stories immensely.

683
00:34:24,545 --> 00:34:26,256
Good. And I enjoyed your, uh...

684
00:34:26,714 --> 00:34:29,634
Well, I learned a lot
about statistical analysis.

685
00:34:30,176 --> 00:34:32,136
- I hope I didn't bore you.
- No, not at all.

686
00:34:32,303 --> 00:34:34,472
Um, in fact, would you like
to go out again?

687
00:34:35,890 --> 00:34:38,059
I would.
Uh... I would. Do...

688
00:34:39,018 --> 00:34:42,397
You are an attractive
and stable character.

689
00:34:42,981 --> 00:34:44,357
Thank you.

690
00:34:44,482 --> 00:34:46,567
But I don't need to conduct an analysis

691
00:34:46,693 --> 00:34:48,861
to see that you're still in love
with someone else.

692
00:34:50,405 --> 00:34:52,782
The probability of us having
a successful relationship

693
00:34:52,907 --> 00:34:55,451
- is no more than 20%.
- 20%?

694
00:34:55,868 --> 00:34:57,245
Tops.

695
00:34:59,497 --> 00:35:01,874
Where is everybody?
I thought we were working the night shift.

696
00:35:02,917 --> 00:35:04,961
Oh, hi. Everybody's out
catching the killer.

697
00:35:05,378 --> 00:35:06,754
Seriously?

698
00:35:06,879 --> 00:35:08,256
No, I'm joking.

699
00:35:08,589 --> 00:35:11,467
That was sarcasm.
Uh, they're out catching the killer.

700
00:35:17,307 --> 00:35:18,683
Nilda, will you hurry up?

701
00:35:18,891 --> 00:35:21,185
- What about Chuy?
- Forget the lizard already!

702
00:35:21,436 --> 00:35:22,812
Essential items only.

703
00:35:24,647 --> 00:35:26,941
Come on! Hurry!

704
00:35:32,447 --> 00:35:33,823
What this?

705
00:35:38,202 --> 00:35:39,579
It's the Caveman.

706
00:35:40,288 --> 00:35:41,831
This is all a big mistake.

707
00:35:42,165 --> 00:35:43,624
We never meant to copy you!

708
00:35:44,167 --> 00:35:46,252
We just took the bodies
to the first place we thought of.

709
00:35:46,377 --> 00:35:47,754
Nothing to do with you, I swear.

710
00:35:47,879 --> 00:35:49,255
We had no idea!

711
00:35:49,797 --> 00:35:51,174
Please don't do this!

712
00:36:00,224 --> 00:36:01,934
I'm thinking this has to do
with your natural soda.

713
00:36:02,935 --> 00:36:05,813
- Something not so natural in it?
- Well, that's no secret.

714
00:36:06,356 --> 00:36:09,067
A lot of foods require additives
to keep them fresh.

715
00:36:09,525 --> 00:36:11,778
Fernanda was giving her friend,
Valerie Bestin

716
00:36:11,903 --> 00:36:13,780
crates of soda to distribute
to the homeless, right?

717
00:36:14,989 --> 00:36:16,366
Yes.

718
00:36:16,949 --> 00:36:20,370
Then a man at the shelter,
Martin Brent, known as Marty

719
00:36:20,495 --> 00:36:22,205
died after drinking the soda, right?

720
00:36:23,373 --> 00:36:25,625
- I don't know any Marty.
- Sure you do.

721
00:36:26,000 --> 00:36:28,836
I'm only telling you this
so you know not to tell us stories.

722
00:36:29,545 --> 00:36:31,798
Why would I lie about knowing
some homeless guy?

723
00:36:32,382 --> 00:36:33,758
The assumption was that Marty died

724
00:36:33,883 --> 00:36:35,468
because his system was too weak
to fight off the flu.

725
00:36:37,053 --> 00:36:39,472
But when Fernanda got sick,
she started to do some digging.

726
00:36:39,806 --> 00:36:41,557
You can't tie any of that to us.

727
00:36:41,682 --> 00:36:43,267
A simple blood test will show
just what additives

728
00:36:43,518 --> 00:36:44,894
were present in his system.

729
00:36:45,937 --> 00:36:48,231
Everyone at the shelter
can confirm drinking the sodas.

730
00:36:48,439 --> 00:36:49,899
Who gave them out
can be tested as well.

731
00:36:51,734 --> 00:36:55,988
Esther said the soda
was losing too much flavor

732
00:36:56,864 --> 00:36:59,534
so we started using glycol
as a stabilizer.

733
00:37:00,410 --> 00:37:02,870
- We got a bad batch.
- How bad?

734
00:37:04,539 --> 00:37:08,793
Esther said you had to drink a lot of it
in order for there to be any bad effects.

735
00:37:09,377 --> 00:37:11,421
Most people just got an upset stomach.

736
00:37:11,712 --> 00:37:13,381
It was only like 500 bottles.

737
00:37:13,965 --> 00:37:16,634
You knew you were endangering
the lives of your consumers.

738
00:37:18,845 --> 00:37:21,556
Esther said pulling the product
was too expensive

739
00:37:22,140 --> 00:37:23,850
and kept saying it was no big deal.

740
00:37:24,934 --> 00:37:27,228
Who drinks 12 bottles in one go anyway?

741
00:37:27,353 --> 00:37:30,398
Right, and then the homeless man,
Marty, died.

742
00:37:31,274 --> 00:37:34,026
Fernanda made the connection,
started asking questions.

743
00:37:37,321 --> 00:37:42,160
Fernanda wouldn't let it go,
said we had to issue a massive recall.

744
00:37:42,702 --> 00:37:44,078
She'd tell if we didn't.

745
00:37:44,495 --> 00:37:45,955
And then you killed your own cousin.

746
00:37:53,296 --> 00:37:54,755
Yeah, we did.

747
00:37:59,969 --> 00:38:03,139
And then Valerie showed up a week
later, and you knew what you had to do.

748
00:38:05,933 --> 00:38:09,770
I told Esther.
That lady didn't know anything.

749
00:38:11,439 --> 00:38:13,191
But she kept saying someone like her

750
00:38:13,316 --> 00:38:16,110
wouldn't stop coming around
and asking questions.

751
00:38:18,404 --> 00:38:19,780
So she had to go.

752
00:38:23,159 --> 00:38:27,163
Fernanda was my niece.
She was supposed to stick up for her own.

753
00:38:28,372 --> 00:38:31,876
But she chose that bitch
on the bus over her family.

754
00:39:04,575 --> 00:39:07,495
Thank you, man. I appreciate it. Really.

755
00:39:08,829 --> 00:39:10,206
It's just soda.

756
00:39:10,331 --> 00:39:11,707
- It's diet, right?
- Yep.

757
00:39:16,003 --> 00:39:17,672
- Mr. Jane?
- Ms. Montague.

758
00:39:17,797 --> 00:39:19,340
We have some important business
to conclude.

759
00:39:19,924 --> 00:39:21,300
We do?

760
00:39:25,721 --> 00:39:27,098
For you.

761
00:39:29,934 --> 00:39:31,310
- I...
- Hang on.

762
00:39:34,188 --> 00:39:35,565
Yeah, that's good.

763
00:39:35,690 --> 00:39:37,066
- Okay?
- Thank you.

764
00:39:37,191 --> 00:39:38,568
I always settle my debts.

765
00:39:38,693 --> 00:39:40,069
You were right.
That wasn't a serial killer.

766
00:39:41,362 --> 00:39:42,738
You remind me. I almost forgot.

767
00:39:45,449 --> 00:39:47,493
Thank you. Forgotten it, too.

768
00:39:47,910 --> 00:39:49,453
Useful brooch. Um, and I...

769
00:39:49,787 --> 00:39:52,498
I was hoping that, you know,
you might forgive me

770
00:39:52,623 --> 00:39:54,166
any misdirection I might have given you.

771
00:39:55,376 --> 00:39:56,752
Oh, yes. No.

772
00:39:57,378 --> 00:40:00,298
No, I... actually, it was bracing

773
00:40:00,423 --> 00:40:02,258
to have one's operating principles
challenged

774
00:40:02,383 --> 00:40:04,302
so... robustly.

775
00:40:04,969 --> 00:40:06,345
Glad to be of service.

776
00:40:06,470 --> 00:40:08,556
You know, I've never participated
in chicanery before.

777
00:40:08,681 --> 00:40:12,852
I can't condone it as a system
but I must admit that it adds...

778
00:40:13,394 --> 00:40:15,021
Zest to the task at hand.

779
00:40:15,980 --> 00:40:17,356
In fact, w...

780
00:40:23,654 --> 00:40:26,157
How did you figure it out,
that it was Esther and Nilda?

781
00:40:29,076 --> 00:40:30,453
Just a good guess.

782
00:40:32,038 --> 00:40:35,249
When we first met with them
they were masking shame, not sadness.

783
00:40:36,834 --> 00:40:39,587
And who likes lizards?
Cold-blooded people.

784
00:40:39,879 --> 00:40:41,255
Lizards.

785
00:40:41,672 --> 00:40:45,259
- I think I shall stick with hard numbers.
- I have another guess for you.

786
00:40:45,635 --> 00:40:49,430
The Caveman isn't coming back.
He is almost certainly dead.

787
00:40:49,889 --> 00:40:52,975
Possibly. I have no personal
bias on the matter.

788
00:40:53,392 --> 00:40:55,811
The numbers will eventually
reveal the truth either way.

789
00:40:56,479 --> 00:40:57,855
They always do.

790
00:40:58,314 --> 00:40:59,690
- You really believe that?
- Of course.

791
00:41:00,399 --> 00:41:01,776
If you could stand
far enough back

792
00:41:01,901 --> 00:41:04,695
to see the big picture,
everything is numbers.

793
00:41:06,072 --> 00:41:07,448
Speaking of which...

794
00:41:08,199 --> 00:41:10,660
You gave me a gift,
so I must give you one.

795
00:41:11,202 --> 00:41:13,037
I ran Red John through
the modeling program.

796
00:41:14,455 --> 00:41:16,832
You might find some
of the data sets useful.

797
00:41:19,627 --> 00:41:22,838
- Wow. Lot of figures and graphs here.
- That's right.

798
00:41:30,012 --> 00:41:31,389
Good luck.

799
00:41:32,598 --> 00:41:33,974
Well, thank you.

